# XNU patches for this board and profile.
#
# All four are compatibility fixes for building xnu-8796 with a current Xcode,
# not behaviour changes. They are device-local copies because the shared
# arch/soc/os series targets xnu-10063; each hunk applies unchanged to
# xnu-8796's sources.

# make_symbol_aliasing.sh looks availability.pl up under ${SDKROOT} in
# xnu-8796. The build driver already installs that file into its fakeroot and
# exports XNU_AVAILABILITY_PL, but only xnu-10063's generator honours the
# variable. Without this the installhdrs pass dies with
# "Unable to locate ....sdk//usr/local/libexec/availability.pl".
local-availability.patch

# makedefs/MakeInc.def passes
# -enable-trivial-auto-var-init-zero-knowing-it-will-be-removed-from-clang,
# the acknowledgement flag Apple's clang needed while the feature was
# experimental. Current clang rejects it as an unknown argument, which fails
# every C and C++ compile. -ftrivial-auto-var-init=zero remains, so the
# behaviour is unchanged.
drop-trivial-auto-var-init-acknowledgement.patch

# The iig code generator lives in the SDK (makedefs/MakeInc.cmd resolves it with
# `xcrun --find iig`), and a current one emits IORPC initialisers that set
# kernelContent inside #ifdef KERNEL. xnu-8796's IORPC has no such field, so
# every DriverKit .iig.cpp fails with "no member named 'kernelContent'".
# A matching generator cannot be built: XNU does not publish iig's source, so
# this backports the one field newer XNU has, with the same guard and type.
backport-iorpc-kernel-content.patch

# A char array element whose size is not a multiple of its alignment is now a
# hard error in clang, and the stack buffers uio_createwithbuffer() fills were
# declared as `uio_stackbuf_t buf[UIO_SIZEOF(n)]` where the element type is an
# aligned char. The declarations now name the alignment themselves, which keeps
# the buffer size exact; widening the element instead would multiply every such
# buffer on the stack by the alignment.
align-uio-stackbuf-declarations.patch

# pmap.h declares reserved10 twice when a platform has neither Rosetta nor
# Apple PAC: once as the CONFIG_ROSETTA fallback and once as the HAS_APPLE_PAC
# fallback. Apple's own arm64 platforms always have PAC, so upstream never saw
# it; newer XNU renumbers the second one. This is the same rename.
rename-pmap-non-pac-padding.patch

# doconf builds the kernel configuration from the '#  NAME = [ ... ]' lines in
# config/MASTER.<cpu> (it extracts them with sed, so the leading '#' is the file
# convention, not a comment). bcm2837 lists neither nos_arm_asm nor nos_arm_pmap,
# and without them osfmk/conf/files.arm64 excludes locore.s, start.s,
# caches_asm.s and the amcc sources - every arm64 boot, cache and copyio symbol
# then comes up undefined at link. Newer XNU and the shared UniXNU machine-layer
# patch both enable them here.
enable-nos-arm-asm.patch

# Enabling nos_arm_asm also makes osfmk/conf/files.arm64 ask for
# osfmk/arm64/amcc_rorgn_ppl.c and amcc_rorgn_common.c, Apple's memory cache
# controller sources. xnu-8796 ships only amcc_rorgn.h - both .c files are gone
# from this version - so the build fails on sources it cannot find. Gating them
# behind an apple_amcc option that no config enables keeps them out of a
# non-Apple kernel, which is what the shared UniXNU machine-layer patch does.
gate-apple-amcc.patch

# osfmk/arm/pmap/pmap.c includes <arm64/ppl/sart.h> and <arm64/ppl/uat.h> and
# calls sart_bootstrap(), uat_bootstrap(), ptep_get_iommu() and the
# pmap_ppl_lockdown/unlockdown pair. All of that belongs to Apple's protected
# page layer, which Apple publishes nowhere: not in the source trees (neither
# 8796 nor 10063 has osfmk/arm64/ppl), not in the Xcode SDK, not in a Kernel
# Development Kit, and not in any device repository.
#
# Rather than fake the headers, this guards the dependency out for a platform
# that has no PPL monitor: the two includes and their bootstrap calls sit behind
# XNU_MONITOR_ANS2_SART / XNU_MONITOR_UAT_PPL, the code-signing lockdown pair
# sits behind PMAP_CS_PPL_MONITOR || PMAP_CS_INCLUDE_CODE_SIGNING, ptep_get_iommu
# is replaced by the page-table entry it formats, and pmap_phys_write_disable
# gets the forward declaration its call site needs. This is the shared UniXNU
# non-Apple IOMMU bootstrap patch, carried device-locally; it applies unchanged
# to xnu-8796.
#
# The kernel therefore builds without the protected page layer, which the
# porting record states as a limit.
non-apple-iommu-bootstrap.patch

# start.s includes <arm64/tunables/tunables.s> and applies per-SoC CPU tunables
# read from MIDR_EL1. The tunables tree is Apple's: it is generated per Apple
# SoC and is not part of the open source release (osfmk/arm64/tunables does not
# exist in either 8796 or 10063). The shared apple-only-cpu-tunables patch guards
# both behind APPLE_ARM64_ARCH_FAMILY, which is exactly right, but its context
# does not match this version - 8796 has an extra #include "assym.s" and a
# different HAS_CLUSTER guard - so this is the same guard adapted to 8796.
apple-only-cpu-tunables-8796.patch

# Enabling nos_arm_asm also asks for osfmk/arm64/kpc.c, Apple's kernel
# performance counters implementation, which this release does not ship (it does
# exist in 10063). The kpc option is part of PERF_DBG_BASE in the bcm2837
# configuration, so the entry is live here. Gating it behind apple_kpc, which no
# configuration enables, keeps it out - the same shape as the amcc gate.
gate-apple-kpc.patch
